anic waste it actually  breaks down into methane which is a  potent greenhouse gas  two-thirds of all waste in california is  organic and the state sees some 5.5  million tons of food waste end up in  landfills each year this new initiative  aims to dramatically reduce that so when  we reach our 75 goal of diverting  organic waste from landfills it is the  equivalent of taking more than a million  cars off the road for over a year since  landfills remain one of the largest  sources of methane gas production one of  the main goals of the new law is to  reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 40  percent by the year 2030 and composting  isn't the only factor the state sees as  success a unique component of the law is  a requirement by businesses to recover  20 percent of the edible food waste that  might otherwise go into a landfill or a  compost bin from restaurants from  retailers such as grocery stores anytime  that they have food that they may have  thrown out before we want to first do a  do a look at it and see if we can  recover some of that food for edible  consumption but after a little more than  a month adjusting to the new laws some  food vendors are still trying to find  their footing we have  many issues the covet shutting down that  that seems to be probably one of the  least  concerning things that i have on my  plate but if they can provide it and  they can teach us how to do it great the  california department of resources and  recycling director says they know it  will take some time for the program to  become part of everyday life but they  are working on making it as seamless as  possible across the state we are giving  grants to local governments from  everything from the startup of their  collection partner to these educational  campaigns  joy has already seen the benefits of  composting and is proud that her state  has now made it law to do what she's  been doing for years i can definitely  say this is the one thing that makes me  very happy to be f