  reported around the world including six  cases in johnson and johnson's own  coveted shot trial still it's a rare  ramification and experts agree the  benefits of the vaccine far outweigh the  risk matt willis is boosted but it was  after his bout with covid in november he  says he too noticed the high-pitched  sound of tinnitus had become more  intense i have to find ways to try and  concentrate  and i'm not fixating on the  on the on the sound the whole time  drowning out the noise and learning to  live with a condition with no cure jamie  morrocker global news toronto  still ahead california's new plan to  keep edible food out of landfills  welcome back a stark reminder tonight of  canada's environmental impact this is  earth overshoot day the annual occasion  marked in canada the united arab  emirates and the united states it's the  day humanity has consumed more natural  resources than the planet can replenish  in a given year it means that just two  and a half months into 2022 we've  already overshot earth's ability to  replenish natural resources  composting and food waste recycling  programs are commonplace here in canada  diverting organic waste from landfills  helps to reduce methane emissions that  contribute to climate change but in the  united states those composting programs  are more limited california is now  hoping to change that by recovering  edible food before it gets tossed in the  trash rob malcolm explains  for the first time mandatory food  recycling has begun in california and  joy kleinberg a mother of five in the  city of davis is already ahead of the  game we've been doing this for at least  five plus years  california is now the first state in the  country to mandate food waste reduction  for businesses and residents alike sb  1383 is the biggest change to our trash  since we started recycling in the 1980s  the move was made in an effort to reduce  greenhouse gas emissions from landfills  what happens when waste goes to a  landfill organic waste it actually  breaks down into methane which is a  potent greenhouse gas  two-thirds of all waste in california is  organic and the state sees some 5.5  million tons of food waste end up in  landfills each year this new initiative  aims to dramatically reduce that so when  we reach our 75 goal of diverting  organic waste from landfills it is the  equivalent of taking more than a million  cars off the road for over a year since  landfills remain one of the largest  sources of methane gas production one of  the main goals of the new law is to  reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 40  percent by the year 2030 and composting  isn't the only factor the state sees as  success a unique component of the law is  a requirement by businesses to recover  20 percent of the edible food waste that  might otherwise go into a landfill or a  compost bin from restaurants from  retailers such as grocery stores anytime  that they have food that they may have  thrown out before we want to first do a  do a look at it and see if we can  recover some of that food for edible  consumption but after a little more than  a month adjusting to the new laws some  food vendors are still trying to find  their footing we have  many issues the covet shutting down that  that seems to be probably one of the  least  concerning things that i have on my  plate but if they can provide it and  they can teach us how to do it great the  california department of resources and  recycling director says they know it  will take some time for the program to  become part of everyday life but they  are working on making it as seamless as  possible across the state we are giving  grants to local governments from  everything from the startup of their  collection partner to these educational  campaigns  joy has already seen the benefits of  composting and is proud that her state  has now made it law to do what she's  been doing for years i can definitely  say this is the one thing that makes me  very happy to be from california  rob malcolm global news sacramento  up next remembering the legacy and  sacrifices of canada's black battalion  the russian invasion of ukraine offers a  grim reminder of how wars can devastate  families communities and entire  countries but in canada there's a new  effort underway to recognize sacrifices  from more than a century ago  contributions that have been largely  overlooked until now ross lord has that  story  despite a topic steeped in racial  prejudice jerome downey points with  pride this is the late private george  alexander down he's from halifax his  great grandfather a veteran of both  world wars and a member of a remarkable  first world war unit the number two  construction battalion men who were  prevented from fighting in the war  because they were black they persevered  forming what became known as the black  battalion building rail lines trenches  and bridges for the soldiers they were  prohibited from fighting alongside  and creating a path of opportunity for  their descendants like army reservist  downey to be a graduate of university to  be a tradesman to be a business owner to  have the opportunities that i have  because of the sacrifices of members of  number two construction battalion means  a lot a connection reinforced by his  recent visit to the azura region of  france where the number two battalion's  efforts are memorialized at a  commonwealth gravesite soldiers who were  overlooked in canada for more than a  century but this summer the federal  government will issue an apology for the  racism that kept battalion members from  being full partners in the war effort  with growing awareness there are new  discoveries that we've just received in  our collection this violin donated to  nova scotia's black cultural center was  hand crafted by a soldier who'd been  part of the battalion's musical band  there's so much history that's hidden  and i think this is a great opportunity  to bring that to the forefront to ensure  that the legacy of the number two  continues on a legacy of determination  and sacrifice qualities everyone can  admire this isn't a black canadian story  this is a canadian story this is our  history that everyone can gravitate to  and hold too proud ross lord global news  dartmouth nova scotia and that is global  national for this sunday night i'm jeff  semple tonight we leave you with some  inspiring moments and passion from  canadian athletes at the paralympic  games in beijing which just wrapped up  today team canada put on its second best  showing ever at a paralympic winter  games winning 25 medals eight gold six  silver and 11 bronze an impressive haul  congratulations to team canada and thank  you for watching have a great night
 

